Algorithm 1. Lines 9-15 correspond to the aggregation stage, which is almost identical to the batch inference algorithm. Note that in Lines 12 and 13, the representation at iteration k of any node in set Bk can be computed, because its representation at iteration k − 1 and the representations of its sampled neighbors at iterationk − 1 have already been computed in the previous loop. The algorithm thus avoids computing the representations for nodes that are not in the current minibatch and not used during the current iteration of stochastic gradient descent. We use the notation Nk(u) to denote a deterministic function which specifies a random sample of a node’s neighborhood (i.e., the randomness is assumed to be pre-computed in the mappings). We index this function byk to denote the fact that the random samples are independent across iterations overk.

